WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.440
Yippee ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm talking Rampage deleted scenes

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.260
and rejected concepts plus an alternate ending
and a post-credits scene we never got to see.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.060
If you're new to my channel and enjoy this,
be sure to subscribe and ring the bell to

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.450
get all my new movie breakdowns and deleted
scenes videos.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:22.750
Obviously, spoilers ahead for the movie, so
take care if you haven't it yet.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.290
Let's kick things off with the original ending
to Rampage which was going to finish on a

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:31.300
rather sad note with the albino silverback
George sacrificing himself to save the day.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.450
When the movie's star Dwayne Johnson read
the original script, his reaction was "Did

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:36.450
I miss something?

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.000
George can't be dead."

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.600
Speaking to Rolling Stone, Johnson explained
that he doesn't like sad endings because "Life

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.260
brings that [and] I don't want it in my movies.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.820
When the credits roll, I want to feel great.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.400
To try and win Johnson over, the movie's producers
took him through all the reasons they thought

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.970
the gorilla should die, saying that he would
be sacrificing himself "like a brave soldier"

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.810
to save the world and kill the animals who
would harm humans.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:05.140
However, Johnson wasn't impressed and reminded
the producers that they weren't "making Saving

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.400
Private Ryan", but a movie with "a crocodile
the size of a football stadium".

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:14.060
Still, it took about two months for the film's
production to resolve the issue and they eventually

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.690
settled on a compromise which you can see
in the final movie when George plays a prank

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.909
on Johnson's character Davis by pretending
to be dead.

00:01:21.909 --> 00:01:26.350
I think the fact that Johnson managed to change
the ending to the film speaks to the power

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.960
that the wrestler-turned-actor has in Hollywood.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.710
In fact, it made me think of Frank Sinatra
who started out as a singer, but whose increasing

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:37.250
influence in 1950s Hollywood allowed him to
change the ending of his film Young At Heart.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.120
As for Johnson, it's clear that he knows his
public very well.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.600
Indeed, he's said that, "I have a relationship
with an audience around the world […] For

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.650
years I've built a trust with them that they're
gonna come to my movies and feel good.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:53.480
So every once in a while, you have to drop
this card, which is [...] we need to figure

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.880
something out, otherwise I'm not gonna do
the movie."

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.500
In the end, Rampage's director Brad Peyton
agreed that Johnson's ending was better and

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.770
said that The Rock "understands the audience
and his relationship with the audience better

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:06.770
than anyone."

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:11.829
So, at the end of Rampage, George is still
alive, but he's too big to go back to San

00:02:11.829 --> 00:02:16.709
Diego Zoo and as the Rock and George head
off, you might be thinking that this is gearing

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:18.219
up a sequel.

00:02:18.219 --> 00:02:19.370
And you'd be right.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:24.520
In fact, Brad Peyton actually filmed a post-credits
sequence that was going to set up a potential

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:25.599
Rampage 2.

00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:30.209
It was a scene that the director really liked
and it also included a cameo from his girlfriend's

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:35.030
parents, however, in the end, the filmmakers
chose not to include it as they didn't want

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:38.739
to tie down the storyline of a future sequel
at this stage.

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:42.930
Peyton has said he's hoping the scene will
be included on the Blu-ray and DVD release

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:43.930
though.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.760
Something that fans of the original Rampage
games might have missed seeing in the movie

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.319
was mutated humans.

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:54.889
In the games, it's typically human characters
who mutate into the monsters via experiments.

00:02:54.889 --> 00:02:58.540
Including mutated humans in the story was
initially considered for the movie though

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.170
it only got as far as the pitch phase, the
reason being that director Brad Peyton says

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:07.889
he found it "hard to relate to" and just couldn't
see how it would work in the final film.

00:03:07.889 --> 00:03:11.870
Ultimately, he felt that concept would have
turned the film into something like a "werewolf

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:14.180
movie" which is not what he wanted.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:18.019
Speaking to Bloody Disgusting, Peyton said
"Tone is a big thing for me.

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:19.809
I want these movies to be fun.

00:03:19.809 --> 00:03:24.219
People use the word absurd, but I also think
it's important to ground them slightly so

00:03:24.219 --> 00:03:26.469
that some of the emotion can be real."

00:03:26.469 --> 00:03:31.549
It seems that for Peyton, then, mutated humans
was one step too far for the crazy movie universe

00:03:31.549 --> 00:03:32.579
of Rampage.

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:37.290
Another change from the video game was making
George an albino gorilla rather than brown

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.019
as he is in most of the games.

00:03:39.019 --> 00:03:42.650
The reason for this change was principally
to make sure that George and Rampage felt

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.480
different to other movies in the genre such
as King Kong and Planet of the Apes.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.669
Although, initially, there were concerns that
it might be hard to get the look of an albino

00:03:50.669 --> 00:03:55.329
gorilla right and make him appear badass enough,
in the end, the filmmakers felt that the design

00:03:55.329 --> 00:03:57.760
choice was a striking and effective one.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.529
Now, in the last scene, after Davis tells
George that he and Naomie Harris's character

00:04:02.529 --> 00:04:06.930
Dr Kate Caldwell are friends, let's just say
that George asks if that friendship comes

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:11.819
with any benefits, at which point Davis tells
George that he should have some class.

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:15.640
However, while there's no real romantic scene
between Harris and Johnson's characters in

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.330
the final movie, one was actually filmed!

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:22.790
Yes, Naomie Harris has confirmed that a kissing
scene between her and Johnson was cut from

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:23.790
the movie.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:27.810
In fact, when InStyle asked Harris what it
was like shooting that, she went so far as

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:32.870
to say that, compared to her other on-screen
kisses, The Rock has the best lips in Hollywood!

